        823.0,
        1352.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "This approach is often used in place of a direct power calculation, but just what is its actual power? From Equations A5.4c and A5.7b, this expression yields the $n$ which gives a test whose power satisfies $z_{(1-\\beta)}=0$ or $\\beta=0.5$. Thus, designs using this sample size have only 50% power, i.e., they are expected to detect an effect only half the time.",
      "source_page": 885,
